  {
    "episode_id": 88914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Listen, first of all, I just think it's important to emphasize that you can't lead the people unless you love the people, which means you got to know the people, spend time with the people listening to the people. And folks are struggling. As we pull our way back from this pandemic, they're dealing with rising costs, which is why I've had laser focus on this issue. It's the reason why I cap the cost of insulin. What I would like to do as you talk about moving into the new cycle is to get that insulin cap, which I've got for folks on Medicare, the people who have private insurance as well. Insulin shouldn't be expensive. It's 100 year old drug. The patent was sold for $1. I would like to provide a federal glide path to help Georgia to do what it should have done a long time ago. Finally expand Medicaid. We've got 600,000 Georgians who are in the healthcare coverage gap. The bulk of these people worked. They're the working poor. My opponent said that if you are well, if you're able-bodied and you have a job, you have healthcare. So he can't even see the people he says he wants to serve. Most of the people in the gap work every single day. I'd like to try to help them to get healthcare to enable them to work and to be healthy. And then we've got to keep creating jobs and opportunity all across our state by investing in domestic manufacturing, continuing to strengthen our technical colleges and community colleges and vocational schools along with four year institutions that prepare the workforce of the future.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Insulin is a 100-year-old drug.",
