    "turn_text": "One of the final questions I have goes back to, as you know, my activism as a related to the John Lewis Bill, I use that as an umbrella term, the Voting Rights Bill. And I'm somewhat, you got to help me with the math here. And I'm saying this because I'm not, I tell people, look, I'm a supporter of Warnock. I'm not a journalist. I don't have to, I can support anybody. I damn well, please. And I'm looking forward to you winning. Thank you. But so if you win, that means there's 51, that would be 51 votes, is that correct? That's correct. Now, the last time around, as you know, we ran into a problem with two Democrats. Mansion and cinema. I have had the leader on Schumer said, look, Joe, that John Lewis, the voting rights bill, that's going to be top priority, one of the top priorities for me. How does that work? In other words, because some people are saying, well, no, you still got a problem, particularly if cinema and mansion maintain the same position they did the last time around. Can you help us understand the math and what it will take to get it passed?",
